<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murad III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1546">1546</a>–<a href="/years/1595">1595</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murad III was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from 1574 until his death in 1595. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> in 1546 to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-ii">Selim II</a> and Nurbanu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, he had his five younger brothers strangled on his accession, and his reign saw the growing influence of the imperial harem, above all his mother and his favourite consort Safiye Sultan. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, a war of 1578–1590 that brought temporary gains, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> was for a time a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> until 1582. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> from the New World brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and unrest among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, while relations with Elizabethan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> were cemented and he commissioned illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> such as the Siyer-i-Nebi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Michael the Brave, from 1593, attacked <b>Murad III</b>'s troops north and south of the Danube with Sigismund Báthory of Transylvania and Aron Vodă of Moldavia, the campaign of the Battle of Călugăreni.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…limited production and helped members in hard times, and through which the state watched over the bazaars and the townspeople; by the 1580s the guilds were a fixture of town life, as the account of the great procession of 1582 for the circumcision of <b>Murad III</b>’s son shows.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
